(x)\0A/// The nearest whole number, halves AWAY FROM ZERO: `round(2.5)` is\0A/// `3.0` and `round(-2.5)` is `-3.0`.\0A///\0A/// `Float.to_fixed` rounds halves to EVEN, which is not an\0A/// inconsistency — it is what C, Python, Rust and Java all do, because\0A/// rounding is arithmetic and formatting is rendering.\0Apub let Float.round (x: Float): Float = round_raw(x)\0A\0A/// A float in `[0.0, 1.0)` from a process-wide generator seeded from\0A/// the clock.\0A///\0A/// Different every run, and NOT reproducible. `Rng.from_seed` is the\0A/// one for a test or a replay.\0Apub let Float.random (): Float = random_raw(true)\0A/// A float in `[lo, hi)`, from the same unseeded generator as\0A/// `Float.random`.\0Apub let Float.random_range (lo: Float) (hi: Float): Float = lo + (hi - lo) * Float.random(())\0A\0A/// --- Trigonometry, logs and constants (issue #20) ---\0A///\0A/// Angles are RADIANS everywhere, which is what libm takes and what the\0A/// inverse functions return. `Float.radians` and `Float.degrees` convert\0A/// at the edges, so a program that thinks in degrees converts once\0A/// rather than scattering `* 3.14159 / 180.0`.\0A/// Sine of an angle in RADIANS.\0Apub let Float.sin (x: Float): Float = sin_raw(x)\0A/// Cosine of an angle in radians.\0Apub let Float.cos (x: Float): Float = cos_raw(x)\0A/// Tangent of an angle in radians. Grows without bound near odd\0A/// multiples of pi/2 rather than failing.\0Apub let Float.tan (x: Float): Float = tan_raw(x)\0A/// The angle in radians whose sine is `x`, in `[-pi/2, pi/2]`. NaN\0A/// outside `[-1, 1]`.\0Apub let Float.asin (x: Float): Float = asin_raw(x)\0A/// The angle in radians whose cosine is `x`, in `[0, pi]`. NaN\0A/// outside `[-1, 1]`.\0Apub let Float.acos (x: Float): Float = acos_raw(x)\0A/// The angle in radians whose tangent is `x`. `Float.atan2` is the\0A/// one that knows which quadrant.\0Apub let Float.atan (x: Float): Float = atan_raw(x)\0A\0A/// `atan2(y, x)`, in that order, matching libm, Go, Python\0A/// and Java all use, and the reason it exists: it knows which QUADRANT\0A/// the point is in, which `atan(y / x)` cannot, and it does not divide\0A/// by zero when `x` is 0.\0Apub let Float.atan2 (y: Float) (x: Float): Float = atan2_raw(y, x)\0A\0A/// Natural log. `log2` and `log10` are separate rather than\0A/// `log(x) / log(2.0)`, because libm computes them directly and the\0A/// division loses accuracy at exactly the powers people check.\0A/// The natural logarithm. NaN for a negative argument, and negative\0A/// infinity at zero.\0Apub let Float.log (x: Float): Float = log_raw(x)\0A/// Base-2 logarithm, computed directly rather than as a division —\0A/// which is what keeps `log2(8.0)` exactly `3.0`.\0Apub let Float.log2 (x: Float): Float = log2_raw(x)\0A/// Base-10 logarithm, computed directly. See `Float.log2`.\0Apub let Float.log10 (x: Float): Float = log10_raw(x)\0A/// `e` raised to `x`.\0Apub let Float.exp (x: Float): Float = exp_raw(x)\0A\0A/// Constants as FUNCTIONS, because `Float.` is a type namespace and\0A/// `T.f(x)` is how a name gets into it. Written out to the last digit a\0A/// double can hold rather than computed, so they are exact literals and\0A/// not a rounding of something else.\0A/// Pi, to the last digit a double holds.\0Apub let Float.pi (): Float = 3.141592653589793\0A/// Two pi — a full turn, which is the more useful constant when\0A/// working with angles.\0Apub let Float.tau (): Float = 6.283185307179586\0A/// Euler's number.\0Apub let Float.e (): Float = 2.718281828459045\0A\0A/// Degrees to radians. Every trigonometric function here takes\0A/// radians, so this is the conversion at the edge.\0Apub let Float.radians (degrees: Float): Float = degrees * 3.141592653589793 / 180.0\0A/// Radians to degrees.\0Apub let Float.degrees (radians: Float): Float = radians * 180.0 / 3.141592653589793\0A\0A/// --- Seeded, reproducible random numbers (issue #20) ---\0A///\0A/// `Float.random` reads a process-global generator seeded from the\0A/// clock, which is right for \22give me something different each run\22 and\0A/// useless for a test or a replay. An `Rng` is a VALUE: same seed, same\0A/// sequence, on every platform and every run.\0A///\0A/// Every call returns the NEXT GENERATOR alongside the number, rather\0A/// than mutating in place, so a generator is as ordinary a value as an\0A/// `Int`. Threading it by hand is the cost; being able to replay a game\0A/// from a seed, or write a test that cannot flake, is what it buys. A\0A/// `Ref[Rng]` is the opt-in for in-place update.\0A///\0A/// --- Which generator, and why ---\0A///\0A/// SplitMix64: a small, modern 64-bit generator with a full 2^64\0A/// period. It is fast, has excellent bit diffusion, and is the right\0A/// generator when reproducibility and simple state matter more than\0A/// cryptographic secrecy. The wrapping multiply and logical shifts it\0A/// needs became expressible when `Int.wrapping_mul` and bitwise\0A/// operators landed.\0A///\0A/// **Not for cryptography, keys, tokens or passwords.** It is a\0A/// statistical generator and its entire state is recoverable from two\0A/// outputs.\0Apub struct Rng { state: Int }\0A\0Alet RNG_GAMMA: Int = -7046029254386353131\0Alet RNG_MIX_1: Int = -4658895280553007687\0Alet RNG_MIX_2: Int = -7723592293110705685\0Alet RNG_RANGE: Int = 9223372036854775807\0A\0A/// Any `Int` is a legal seed, including zero and negatives. SplitMix64\0A/// has no forbidden state, unlike generators whose all-zero state gets\0A/// stuck, so the seed is kept exactly rather than folded into a range.\0Apub let Rng.from_seed (seed: Int): Rng = Rng { state: seed }\0A\0Alet plum__mod_floor (a: Int) (m: Int): Int = {\0A    let r = a - (a / m) * m;\0A    if r < 0 { r + m } else { r }\0A}\0A\0A/// The raw step: advance by SplitMix64's golden-ratio increment, then\0A/// mix all 64 bits. Keep the sign bit here: wide integer ranges need\0A/// the full signed domain. Each caller reduces the output as needed.\0Alet rng_step (r: Rng): (Rng, Int) = {\0A    let next = Rng { state: Int.wrapping_add(r.state, RNG_GAMMA) };\0A    let z1 = Int.wrapping_mul(next.state ^ Int.shr_logical(next.state, 30), RNG_MIX_1);\0A    let z2 = Int.wrapping_mul(z1 ^ Int.shr_logical(z1, 27), RNG_MIX_2);\0A    (next, z2 ^ Int.shr_logical(z2, 31))\0A}\0A\0A/// A `Float` in `[0.0, 1.0)`. Use 53 exactly representable bits so\0A/// converting to `Float` cannot round the largest sample up to `1.0`.\0A/// The generator advances exactly once, as for previous versions;\0A/// float samples may differ because the discarded low bits no longer round.\0Apub let Rng.float (r: Rng): (Rng, Float) = {\0A    let (next, z) = rng_step(r);\0A    (next, Int.shr_logical(z & RNG_RANGE, 10).to_float() / 9007199254740992.0)\0A}\0A\0A/// A float in `[lo, hi)`, and the next generator.\0Apub let Rng.float_range (r: Rng) (lo: Float) (hi: Float): (Rng, Float) = {\0A    let (next, f) = Rng.float(r);\0A    (next, lo + (hi - lo) * f)\0A}\0A\0A/// `[lo, hi)`, the upper bound EXCLUDED, so `int_range(r, 0, xs.len())`\0A/// is an index into `xs` and needs no `- 1` anywhere.\0A/// All ordered `Int` bounds are supported, even when `hi - lo` would\0A/// overflow. `hi` must itself be an `Int`, so the maximum `Int` cannot\0A/// be included as a result of this half-open API.\0A///\0A/// Rejection sampling, not `z % span`. The modulo is biased whenever the\0A/// span does not divide the generator's range evenly: the low values\0A/// come up more often, by about one part in `range / span`. That is\0A/// invisible in a game and fatal in a shuffle, and the loop costs an\0A/// extra step only on the fraction of draws that land in the leftover\0A/// tail. Widths through the maximum `Int` preserve their seeded sequences.\0A/// Wider ranges reject full signed samples outside `[lo, hi)` instead;\0A/// they accept at least half of the domain, averaging at most two draws.\0A///\0A/// An empty or backwards range returns `lo` and does not draw, because\0A/// there is no number to return and a panic here would make an ordinary\0A/// off-by-one crash a program at random.\0Apub let Rng.int_range (r: Rng) (lo: Int) (hi: Int): (Rng, Int) = {\0A    if hi <= lo { (r, lo) } else {\0A        let span = Int.wrapping_sub(hi, lo);\0A        if span < 0 { rng_wide(r, lo, hi) } else { rng_below(r, span, lo) }\0A    }\0A}\0A\0A// Tail recursion keeps retries in constant stack space. No span or\0A// offset arithmetic is needed for ranges at least 2^63 wide.\0Alet rng_wide (r: Rng) (lo: Int) (hi: Int): (Rng, Int) = {\0A    let (next, z) = rng_step(r);\0A    if z < lo || z >= hi { rng_wide(next, lo, hi) } else { (next, z) }\0A}\0A\0Alet rng_below (r: Rng) (span: Int) (lo: Int): (Rng, Int) = {\0A    let range = RNG_RANGE;\0A    let limit = range - plum__mod_floor(range, span);\0A    let (next, raw) = rng_step(r);\0A    let z = raw & RNG_RANGE;\0A    if z >= limit { rng_below(next, span, lo) } else { (next, lo + plum__mod_floor(z, span)) }\0A}\0A\0A/// A boolean, evenly, and the next generator.\0Apub let Rng.bool (r: Rng): (Rng, Bool) = {\0A    let (next, v) = Rng.int_range(r, 0, 2);\0A    (next, v == 1)\0A}\0A\0A/// Fisher-Yates, walking from the end so every permutation is equally\0A/// likely. The naive version, swapping each element with any other, is\0A/// not uniform, and looks fine until somebody counts.\0Apub let Rng.shuffle[T] (r: Rng) (xs: Array[T]): (Rng, Array[T]) =\0A    rng_shuffle_acc(r, xs, xs.len() - 1)\0A\0Alet rng_shuffle_acc[T] (r: Rng) (xs: Array[T]) (i: Int): (Rng, Array[T]) =\0A    if i <= 0 { (r, xs) } else {\0A        let (next, j) = Rng.int_range(r, 0, i + 1);\0A        rng_shuffle_acc(next, plum__swap(xs, i, j), i - 1)\0A    }\0A\0Alet plum__swap[T] (xs: Array[T]) (i: Int) (j: Int): Array[T] =\0A    if i == j { xs } else {\0A        let a = xs[i];\0A        let b = xs[j];\0A        xs.set(i, b).set(j, a)\0A    }\0A\0A/// One element, or `None` from an empty array.\0Apub let Rng.choice[T] (r: Rng) (xs: Array[T]): (Rng, Option[T]) =\0A    if Array.is_empty(xs) { (r, None) } else {\0A        let (next, i) = Rng.int_range(r, 0, xs.len());\0A        (next, Some(xs[i]))\0A    }\0A\0A// --- String ordering ---\0A\0A/// Byte-lexicographic, which for UTF-8 IS codepoint order, so this\0A/// agrees with the real prelude's rune-by-rune comparison without\0A/// needing a `.runes()` decoder.\0Alet string_concat_all_raw (parts: Array[String]): String = \22\22\0A\0A/// Joins in ONE allocation. Building a large string by repeated\0A/// `.concat` is quadratic; this is what the compiler's own emitter\0A/// uses for anything that accumulates.\0Apub let Array.concat_all (parts: Array[String]): String = string_concat_all_raw(parts)\0A\0A/// Decimal text to the NEAREST double, via libc `strtod`.\0A///\0A/// `parse_number` below validates the grammar and finds where the\0A/// number ends, and that part was always right. What it got wrong was\0A/// the VALUE: building it as `mantissa * 10^exp` in floating point\0A/// accumulates rounding error, so `9.21258e-07` parsed to a double\0A/// one ulp away and `parse_float(x.to_string()) != x`. `to_string`\0A/// goes to real lengths to guarantee that round trip, retrying\0A/// at 15, 16 and 17 digits until `strtod` agrees, so the inverse\0A/// has to be the same `strtod`, not an approximation of it.\0A///\0A/// Found by a property test on 2026-08-25. The Rust interpreter\0A/// computed the identical wrong double, so `interp-check` agreed\0A/// with it and saw nothing.\0Alet parse_float_raw (s: String): Float = 0.0\0A\0Alet string_cmp_raw (a: String) (b: String): Int = 0\0A\0A/// Sorted by BYTE order, not by any locale's alphabet — so uppercase\0A/// sorts before lowercase, and accented letters sort after `z`.\0Apub let Array.sort_string (arr: Array[String]): Array[String] = Array.sort_by(arr, |a: String, b: String| a <= b)\0A\0A// --- Assertions ---\0A//\0A// Mirrored from the real prelude, message for message: a test that\0A// fails should read the same whichever compiler ran it.\0A\0A/// Stops the program unless `cond` holds.\0A///\0A/// For a condition the code depends on, not for input validation:\0A/// there is no way to catch this.\0Apub let assert (cond: Bool): Unit =\0A    if cond { () } else { panic_raw(\22assertion failed\22) }\0A\0A/// Stops the program unless the two are equal, printing BOTH — which\0A/// is the difference from `assert(a == b)` and the reason to prefer\0A/// it in a test.\0Apub let assert_eq[T] (a: T) (b: T): Unit =\0A    if a == b { () } else {\0A        panic_raw(\22assertion failed: left != right\5Cn  left:  \22.concat(a.to_string()).concat(\22\5Cn  right: \22).concat(b.to_string()))\0A    }\0A\0A/// Stops the program if the two are equal, printing them.\0Apub let assert_ne[T] (a: T) (b: T): Unit =\0A    if a != b { () } else {\0A        panic_raw(\22assertion failed: left == right\5Cn  left:  \22.concat(a.to_string()).concat(\22\5Cn  right: \22).concat(b.to_string()))\0A    }\0A\0A// --- Collections: Map and Set ---\0A\0A/// One key and value stored in a map bucket.\0Apub struct MapEntry[K, V] { key: K, value: V }\0A/// A hash map from `K` to `V`.\0A///\0A/// PERSISTENT: `Map.insert` returns a new map and leaves the original\0A/// alone, so a map can be shared without copying defensively. Keys\0A/// need `Eq` and `Hash`. Equal keys hash identically.\0A/// NaN is not a supported key, including inside aggregates, because\0A/// IEEE equality makes it unequal to itself. References use identity.\0Apub struct Map[K, V] { buckets: Array[Array[MapEntry[K, V]]], size: Int }\0A\0Alet MAP_INITIAL_BUCKETS: Int = 8\0Alet MAP_LOAD_FACTOR_NUM: Int = 3\0Alet MAP_LOAD_FACTOR_DEN: Int = 4\0A\0Alet map_make_buckets[K, V] (n: Int): Array[Array[MapEntry[K, V]]] = {\0A    let mut buckets = [];\0A    for i in 0..n { buckets = buckets.push([]); };\0A    buckets\0A}\0A\0Alet value_hash[T: Hash] (x: T): Int = x.hash()\0A\0Alet map_bucket_index[K: Hash] (k: K) (bucket_count: Int): Int = value_hash(k) % bucket_count\0A\0Alet map_bucket_find_index[K: Eq, V] (bucket: Array[MapEntry[K, V]]) (k: K) (i: Int): Int =\0A    if i >= bucket.len() { -1 }\0A    else if bucket[i].key == k { i }\0A    else { map_bucket_find_index(bucket, k, i + 1) }\0A\0A/// An empty map.\0Apub let Map.new[K, V] (): Map[K, V] = Map { buckets: map_make_buckets(MAP_INITIAL_BUCKETS), size: 0 }\0A\0Alet map_rehash[K: Hash, V] (m: Map[K, V]) (new_bucket_count: Int): Map[K, V] = {\0A    let mut new_buckets = map_make_buckets(new_bucket_count);\0A    for bucket in m.buckets {\0A        for entry in bucket {\0A            let idx = map_bucket_index(entry.key, new_bucket_count);\0A            new_buckets = new_buckets.set(idx, new_buckets[idx].push(entry));\0A        }\0A    };\0A    Map { buckets: new_buckets, size: m.size }\0A}\0A\0A/// A NEW map with `k` set to `v`, replacing any previous value. The\0A/// original is unchanged.\0Apub let Map.insert[K: Eq + Hash, V] (m: Map[K, V]) (k: K) (v: V): Map[K, V] = {\0A    let idx = map_bucket_index(k, m.buckets.len());\0A    let bucket = m.buckets[idx];\0A    let existing_i = map_bucket_find_index(bucket, k, 0);\0A    let inserted = if existing_i >= 0 {\0A        Map { buckets: m.buckets.set(idx, bucket.set(existing_i, MapEntry { key: k, value: v })), size: m.size }\0A    } else {\0A        Map { buckets: m.buckets.set(idx, bucket.push(MapEntry { key: k, value: v })), size: m.size + 1 }\0A    };\0A    if inserted.size * MAP_LOAD_FACTOR_DEN > inserted.buckets.len() * MAP_LOAD_FACTOR_NUM {\0A        map_rehash(inserted, inserted.buckets.len() * 2)\0A    } else {\0A        inserted\0A    }\0A}\0A\0A/// The value for `k`, or `None`.\0Apub let Map.get[K: Eq + Hash, V] (m: Map[K, V]) (k: K): Option[V] = {\0A    let bucket = m.buckets[map_bucket_index(k, m.buckets.len())];\0A    let i = map_bucket_find_index(bucket, k, 0);\0A    if i >= 0 { Some(bucket[i].value) } else { None }\0A}\0A\0A/// Whether `k` has a value.\0Apub let Map.contains[K: Eq + Hash, V] (m: Map[K, V]) (k: K): Bool =